            log line per packet), tools/radwire.py (decodes and counts the RADIUS packets
            in the pcap), tools/qmark.py (reads the CLI help surface), tools/cfg.py (sends
            configuration), tools/radius.py (the shared RADIUS codec). All ran on tb277.

  The server was started with this command:

    tools/tb tool tb277 ckaaa radsrv --bind 10.38.22.33 --auth-port 11812 --acct-port 11813 \
      --secret testing123 --client 10.38.22.34 --client 10.38.22.66 \
      --user radclient1:radpass123 --user manager:friend \
      --reply-attr 6=0x00000006 \
      --reply-attr 26=0x0000000901137368656c6c3a707269762d6c766c3d3135 \
      --log /tmp/ckaaa/radsrv4.log --seconds 2400 --stopfile /tmp/ckaaa/stop4

  `6=0x00000006` is Service-Type = Administrative. Attribute 26 is the vendor attribute
  `shell:priv-lvl=15`. Step 4 explains why both are needed.

STEP 1 -- instrument check: a local login still works                       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: `aaa authentication login` only runs when somebody really logs in. No `show`
  command can exercise it. So a real console login is the only possible instrument.
  conlogin.py logs the console out and back in, and reports what prompt it reached.

tb277$ tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user manager --password friend
    === BASELINE-OFF L1: local login as manager must succeed (instrument check) ===
    LOGIN RESULT: USER  user=manager  seconds=0.4  prompt='awplus>'
    conlogin: expected PRIV, got USER -- MISMATCH
    conlogin: restore login as manager -> USER ('awplus>')
    rc=5
                                           <-- the login worked; only the expectation was wrong

  Measured platform fact: an AW+ console login lands at privilege level 1, with the prompt
  `awplus>`, even for a user defined as `username manager privilege 15`. The command
  `enable` then raises the level to 15, and `show privilege` prints "Current privilege level
  is 15". So on this platform a console login test passes when it reaches `>`, which
  conlogin reports as USER. It does not have to reach `#`.
  => STEP 1 PASS: the local user `manager` logged in on the console in 0.4 s and reached the
     prompt `awplus>`. The login instrument works, and the correct success criterion for the
     later steps is now known.

