  The key is stored and displayed only in encrypted form. The plain text never appears.

  Measured requirement: the Access-Accept must carry an authorization attribute.
  A RADIUS server that answers with an Access-Accept holding only a Reply-Message is not
  enough for this DUT. The DUT accepts the reply and then drops the session:

    awplus login: radclient1
    Password:
    login:
    Session setup problem, abort.

  The server log proves the authentication itself succeeded:

    09:22:50 Access-Request  from 10.38.22.34 id=195 user='radclient1' | Message-Authenticator=0x451a...,
             User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden>, NAS-Identifier='console',
             NAS-Port=20833, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)
    09:22:50 Access-Accept   to 10.38.22.34 id=195 user='radclient1' (ok)

  and the DUT's own log names what then went wrong:

    2026 Oct 19 20:22:36 authpriv.err awplus login: Invalid user name "radclient1". Abort.
    2026 Oct 19 20:22:41 daemon.notice awplus systemd[1]: serial-getty@ttyS0.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=1/FAILURE

  Adding `Service-Type = Administrative (6)` and the vendor attribute `shell:priv-lvl=15` to
  the Access-Accept made the same login succeed, which step 5 shows. This matters to anyone
  repeating the case. The console message "Session setup problem, abort." does not say what
  is missing. What is missing is an authorization attribute in the RADIUS reply.
  => STEP 4 PASS: the DUT is configured as a RADIUS client. It reports the server as Alive
     on both the authentication and the accounting port, and the key is stored encrypted.
     swi_d.cfg was saved at this point.

