  What the DUT puts in its Access-Request, measured and not assumed: a Message-Authenticator,
  `User-Name`, a PAP `User-Password`, `NAS-Identifier='console'`, a `NAS-Port` that changes
  per session, and `Service-Type = 8 (Authenticate-Only)`. It does not send NAS-IP-Address.

  A second measured detail on the DUT console: a login served by RADIUS prints no
  `Last login:` line, while a local login always prints one. Five local logins in this case
  printed it. Three RADIUS-served logins did not. There was no exception. The reason is
  plain: `Last login:` is read from the Linux lastlog of a local account, and a
  RADIUS-authenticated session has no local account to read. This is offered as a
  corroborating sign that is visible on the DUT alone. The server log and the wire capture
  stay the authoritative instruments.
  => STEP 5 PASS: a user who exists only on the RADIUS server logged in to the DUT in 0.6 s.
     Three independent views agree: the console reached `awplus>`, the server logged
     Access-Request id=228 and Access-Accept id=228, and the wire shows the same pair with a
     valid authenticator. Accounting is paired: a Start on login and a Stop on logout, both
     carrying Acct-Session-Id '00021146', with Acct-Session-Time on the Stop.

