  Server log:

    09:27:17 Access-Request         from 10.38.22.34 id=254 user='radclient1' | Message-Authenticator=0x65bd7b0815bfed4009cfc707deeea7c2, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden>, NAS-Identifier='console', NAS-Port=21283, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)
    09:27:17 Access-Reject          to 10.38.22.34 id=254 user='radclient1' (wrong password)

  Wire:

  52.284  10.38.22.34:22308 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request       id=254 len=95  Message-Authenticator=0x65bd7b0815bfed4009cfc707deeea7c2, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden,16b>, NAS-Identifier='console', NAS-Port=21283, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)
  52.285  10.38.22.33:11812 -> 10.38.22.34:22308  Access-Reject        id=254 len=46  Reply-Message='Rejected: wrong password'  [authenticator VALID for the given secret]

  Measured behaviour: `local` in the method list is a fallback for an unreachable server,
  not for a Reject. The method list was `aaa authentication login default group radius
  local`. The server answered Access-Reject for the user `manager`, because the test server
  did not know that user. The DUT obeyed the Reject. It did not then try the local database:

    09:22:57 Access-Request from 10.38.22.34 id=134 user='manager' ...
    09:22:57 Access-Reject  to 10.38.22.34 id=134 user='manager' (no such user)
    console: `Login incorrect`

  This follows the RFC: a Reject is authoritative. The practical consequence is important.
  Once the method list points at RADIUS, every account you still need must also exist on the
  RADIUS server. Writing `local` at the end of the list does not protect you. Only two
  patterns are safe: the RADIUS server also knows the administrative account, or the RADIUS
  server is unreachable, where the fallback does work (step 7).
  => STEP 6 PASS: the wrong password produced an Access-Reject from the server and a refused
     login in 1.4 s. The request reached the server, so this is a RADIUS rejection and not a
     local lockout.

