tb277$ tools/tb conlogin tb277 ckaaa u2 115200 --user radclient1 --password radpass123 --settle 90 --expect fail
    === C2: radclient1 with correct password but a MISMATCHED shared key -- expect refusal ===
    LOGIN RESULT: FAIL  user=radclient1  seconds=21.9  prompt='awplus login:'
    conlogin: expected FAIL, got FAIL -- MATCH

  Server log. The requests do arrive, and the server discards each one without answering,
  which is what RFC 3579 requires when the Message-Authenticator does not verify:

    09:27:55 DROP-bad-msg-auth      from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:00 DROP-bad-msg-auth      from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:05 DROP-bad-msg-auth      from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'
    09:28:10 DROP-bad-msg-auth      from 10.38.22.34 user='radclient1'

  Wire. The same request id and the same Message-Authenticator, sent again 5.003 s apart:

  90.220  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request       id=183 len=95  Message-Authenticator=0x427895c597978bdc97aeecf98c95610c, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden,16b>, NAS-Identifier='console', NAS-Port=21334, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)
  95.223  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request       id=183 len=95  Message-Authenticator=0x427895c597978bdc97aeecf98c95610c, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden,16b>, NAS-Identifier='console', NAS-Port=21334, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)
 100.228  10.38.22.34:22359 -> 10.38.22.33:11812  Access-Request       id=183 len=95  Message-Authenticator=0x427895c597978bdc97aeecf98c95610c, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden,16b>, NAS-Identifier='console', NAS-Port=21334, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=8(Authenticate-Only)

  The accounting packets with the wrong key were discarded the same way, through the
  Accounting Request Authenticator. The server logged 4 of them as
  `DROP-bad-acct-auth ... (wrong secret)`.

  One limit of the NAS view. The DUT's own log says `pam_radius_auth: RADIUS server
  10.38.22.33 failed to respond` here, and it says the same in step 7, where the server was
  really gone. A discarded packet and a dead server look the same to a NAS. That is by
  design.
  The DUT's key was set back to `testing123` straight after this step.
  => STEP 8 PASS: the wrong key made the server discard every request. The DUT retried the
     same request 3 times, 5.003 s apart. That measures Timeout 5 sec and Retransmit Count 3
     on the wire, instead of reading them from a `show` command. The login then fell through
     to `local`, where `radclient1` has no account, so it was refused after 21.9 s.

